================================================================================
VI:: EXCEPTION ARCHITECTURE: TWO-TIER GUARD & INVERTED PRINTING POLICY
================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The exception handling architecture enforces absolute domain containment and 
scannable terminal diagnostics. It guarantees that untrusted, third-party, or 
native operating system exceptions never leak into the core application layer, 
and that all process-terminating errors are normalized into a unified, strictly 
typed exception hierarchy (`Fatal` / `Notice`).

The system relies on three distinct operational pillars:
  - Pillar I:   Boundary Guard (Anti-Corruption Layer)
  - Pillar II:  Application Guard (Inside Sweeper)
  - Pillar III: Top-Down Inverted Terminal Presentation


2. PILLAR I: BOUNDARY GUARD (PROTECT_ADAPTER)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All external adapter dependencies, third-party libraries, and bridge calls are 
wrapped dynamically via `ExceptionPolicy.protect_adapter`. 

Key Behaviors:
  - Dynamic Wrapping: Scans target adapter methods at runtime and applies a 
    transparent protective enclosure around every public method.
  - Passthrough Exceptions: Explicitly re-raises intended control flow signals 
    (`Notice` and explicitly adopted notice types).
  - Normalization: Intercepts all arbitrary `Exception` types and re-raises 
    them normalized as `BridgeLeakage` (a `Fatal` subtype) using `from ex` 
    cause-chaining (`raise BridgeLeakage() from ex`).
  - System Signals: Catches standard `Exception` only, ensuring process 
    interrupts (`KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`) pass through unhindered.


3. PILLAR II: APPLICATION GUARD (APPENGINE SWEEPER)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Within the core application boundaries (`AppEngine`), domain operations are 
guarded to manage anticipated business failure modes.

Key Behaviors:
  - Domain Normalization: Converts system or environment state invalidities into 
    explicit, domain-specific `Fatal` subclasses (e.g., `InvalidConfiguration`).
  - Containment: Unhandled internal operational failures are swept and 
    re-raised inside the internal failure hierarchy, ensuring no raw Python 
    runtime exceptions escape the engine boundary.
  - Guarantee: By mathematical construction of Pillar I and Pillar II, any 
    exception escaping the main engine context is guaranteed to inherit from 
    the base `Fatal` exception class.


4. PILLAR III: DYNAMIC FORMATTING & INVERTED PRESENTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Standard Python tracebacks render bottom-up (oldest error first, newest error 
last), which leads to trailing empty exception labels and buried root causes. 
This architecture replaces default printing with a top-down, scannable format 
managed directly by `ExceptionPolicy.print_traceback_and_exit`.

A. Self-Formatting Exception Types (`__str__`)
  - `Fatal` Base Class: Conditionally formats string representations to include 
    a leading colon (`: <message>`) only if a message string exists. If empty, 
    it returns `""` to prevent trailing punctuation artifacts.
  - `BridgeLeakage`: Dynamically inspects its `__cause__` attribute when raised 
    without explicit arguments, deriving a clean, descriptive header string in 
    the format `: [<CauseType>] <CauseMessage>`.

B. Inverted Terminal Presentation
  The terminal printer splits execution into two clear phases:
    1. Failure Header: Immediately prints the top-level failure category and its 
       derived summary string (`[FATAL] <ExceptionClass>: <Details>`).
    2. Cause Traceback Dump: Inspects `ex.__cause__` and renders the exact 
       underlying stack trace directly beneath the failure header, pinpointing 
       the originating line in the adapter layer without noise.


5. DATA FLOW SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[ Native OS / Third-Party Error ]
               │
               ▼  (Interception by Boundary Guard)
[ BridgeLeakage() from ex ]
               │
               ▼  (Bubbles through AppEngine Sweeper)
[ ExceptionPolicy.print_traceback_and_exit(ex) ]
               │
               ├──► 1. Render Header: [FATAL] BridgeLeakage: [ErrnoType] Message
               └──► 2. Render Body:   --- Underlying Stack Trace ---
                                      (Exact line numbers & cause stack)